# BioFusion PQ — Prototype Scaffold

> Windows 11 + Python 3.11. All paths shown are PowerShell-friendly.


---

## 1) Setup (venv + deps)

```powershell
# From the repo root
py -3.11 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -r .\requirements.txt
2) Run ProtoChain devnet
# From the repo root (venv active)
uvicorn src.protochain:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --reload
Run Streamlit lab (app.py)
# From the repo root (venv active)
streamlit run .\src\app.py --server.port 8501
Bulk wallet generation
data\index.sample.csv
path,user_id
data/modalities/user01_face.png,user01
data/modalities/user02_face.png,user02
path,user_id
data/modalities/user01_face.png,user01
data/modalities/user02_face.png,user02
Generate outputs

CSV:
python .\src\bulk_wallets.py --in .\data\index.sample.csv --out .\artifacts\summary.csv --format csv
JSONL:
python .\src\bulk_wallets.py --in .\data\index.sample.csv --out .\artifacts\summary.jsonl --format jsonl
5) Exporting CSV / PDF from Streamlit

CSV (typical patterns):

If the app shows a “Download CSV” button, click it to save the current table/metrics.

If there’s a table meant for export but no button, select the table’s data and paste into Excel, then File → Save As → CSV (quick fallback).

PDF of the app/figures (no code changes):

In your browser on the Streamlit page, press Ctrl+P → Destination: Microsoft Print to PDF → More settings → enable Background graphics → Print.

For only a plot/section, use the element’s context menu (if present) or right-click the image → Open image in new tab → print that tab to PDF.

(If you prefer in-app one-click exports, add a st.download_button for CSV and a savefig('figures/...pdf') call where you create the Matplotlib figures.)